From 52ef926b4eb304f1ae8e2f606d9c00b9a20f412b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 08:13:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Remove tf.compat.v1 from the TensorFlow entry points and
 tests

Seven of the tree's tf.compat.v1 references live outside the network and
training/inference code that the other TF2 patches rewrite: four graph
resets in the evaluation, visualisation and training entry points, one
logging call in the package __init__, and four Session uses in the tests.
None of them needs a v1 shim; each has a supported TF2 spelling that this
patch swaps in, and the behaviour is unchanged.

tf.compat.v1.reset_default_graph() -> tf.keras.backend.clear_session().
The six call sites (core/evaluate.py, core/evaluate_multianimal.py,
visualizemaps.py, training.py) all do the same thing: drop the graph
before building a model, and drop it again between successive snapshots
so ops do not accumulate across an evaluation loop.  clear_session() is
the documented TF2 replacement for exactly that pattern, it calls
reset_default_graph() itself while eager execution is disabled, and it is
already the idiom this codebase uses -- predict_videos.py:223 has called
tf.keras.backend.clear_session() since before any of these patches.  The
only observable difference is that the fresh graph carries the two Keras
learning-phase ops that clear_session() re-creates, which the old code
would have created lazily on the first Keras layer anyway.

tf.compat.v1.logging.set_verbosity(tf.compat.v1.logging.ERROR) ->
tf.get_logger().setLevel(logging.ERROR).  tf.compat.v1.logging is a thin
wrapper over the "tensorflow" Python logger and its level constants are
the logging module's own, so the two forms are the same call.  Measured
after importing deeplabcut.pose_estimation_tensorflow, before and after:
logger level 40, tf.compat.v1.logging.get_verbosity() 40, propagate True,
one StreamHandler; an INFO and a WARNING record are dropped and an ERROR
record is emitted, identically in both.

tf.compat.v1.Session() -> tf.keras.backend.get_value() in
tests/test_predict_multianimal.py and tests/test_tf_install_smoke.py.
find_local_peak_indices_maxpool_nms is built from tf.nn.max_pool2d,
tf.equal and tf.where, so it returns an eager tensor as soon as eager
execution is on and there is nothing for a Session to run.  The old form
was a latent dependency on core/train.py disabling eager execution
process-wide at import: with that call removed, the three
test_predict_multianimal tests fail with "RuntimeError: The Session graph
is empty".  get_value() reads the tensor in either mode, so the tests
pass both as the tree stands today and once eager execution comes back.

Evidence, all on a container with tensorflow 2.15.0 and tf_keras 2.15.0.

evaluate_network on a trained resnet_50 single-animal project and a
trained dlcrnetms5 multi-animal project, before and after: the prediction
h5 files are bit-identical (max abs diff 0.0), and CombinedEvaluation-
results.csv, the per-shuffle results csv, dist_5.csv and the full
detection pickle are byte-identical.  Reported errors are unchanged --
"train error: 309.25 pixels. Test error: 292.41 pixels" both times.

extract_maps on the same project hashes to
d157323bdb3b1d22d42d8265847474c9cc51fff7dfb6a53e3c11b47780580d0b over
three runs before and three runs after with TF_ENABLE_ONEDNN_OPTS=0.
With oneDNN left on, that hash alternates between two values run to run
regardless of which version of the code is installed -- a scoremap sum of
199.099339 versus 199.0993365, 1.3e-8 relative -- so the difference is
oneDNN's own summation order, not this change.

train_network for two iterations on the same project succeeds before and
after, and return_train_network_path returns the same three paths.

The test suite (excluding gui/, which needs Qt, and tools/, which does
not import) gives 3368 passed, 15 failed, 30 skipped, 5 xfailed both
before and after, with the same 15 failures -- missing test data and
pytorch dataset tests, none of them related.
